1
00:00:00,490 --> 00:00:04,360
Hello, everybody, and welcome to our next grammar lesson.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:10,360
So today we are going to talk about comparatives and superlatives.

3
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:15,230
Probably a lot of you are thinking, oh, my God, what is that?

4
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:19,780
But it only sounds difficult. When you know what it is,

5
00:00:19,810 --> 00:00:22,030
it's not so complicated.

6
00:00:22,970 --> 00:00:25,250
So let's start.

7
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:26,930
Okay.

8
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:34,910
So before we cut to the chase, I would like to explain the difference between adjectives and adverbs.

9
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:38,180
We need to understand that difference for

10
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:39,290
for this topic.

11
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:41,360
So.

12
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:43,810
Adjectives.

13
00:00:43,930 --> 00:00:48,580
Adjectives are words that describe people or things.

14
00:00:48,970 --> 00:00:51,670
For example, slow computer.

15
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:54,390
Loud noise.

16
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:59,590
So the question here would be What kind of computer?

17
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:01,330
Slow computer.

18
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:03,520
What kind of noise?

19
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:05,080
Loud noise.

20
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:17,780
So these are the words before nouns describing them. That's adjectives. Now adverbs describe actions.

21
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:20,950
So to work slowly.

22
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:24,090
To speak loudly.

23
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:29,070
And the question here would be To work

24
00:01:29,070 --> 00:01:32,700
how? Slowly. To speak

25
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:35,040
how? Loudly.

26
00:01:36,510 --> 00:01:48,330
Okay, usually we make adverbs with the ending -ly and well, yeah, most often we make them with with that

27
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:48,960
ending.

28
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,190
-ly.

29
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:53,610
And as I said, the question is how?

30
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,900
How something is done - to work slowly, to speak loudly.

31
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:01,640
Okay.

32
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:06,170
So now you understand this and we can move further.

33
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:11,260
So first we'll talk about comparative objectives.

34
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:11,880
Right.

35
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,210
And here are some rules.

36
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:23,780
So comparatives, we use them if we want to compare things.

37
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:31,190
If we want to say that something is bigger and the other thing is smaller.

38
00:02:31,370 --> 00:02:33,380
So these are comparatives.

39
00:02:35,210 --> 00:02:39,560
And as I said, there are certain rules how to make them.

40
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:40,880
Here they are.

41
00:02:41,830 --> 00:02:49,090
So if the world is short, if it's only one syllable - clean.

42
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:51,000
Simple.

43
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:52,950
Only one syllable.

44
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:57,510
We add ending -er here.

45
00:02:58,430 --> 00:02:59,270
Cleaner.

46
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:02,040
This is a cleaner room.

47
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:03,620
Simpler.

48
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:08,370
This task is simpler than that.

49
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:19,470
So pay attention that when the world ends in -e like here, we omit it right

50
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:25,920
When we add this ending -er we just skip the other e, right

51
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:29,730
So we skip the last e and just add this ending

52
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:33,870
-er. Okay, we move on.

53
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,670
What about two syllable words?

54
00:03:37,670 --> 00:03:38,630
A bit longer

55
00:03:38,630 --> 00:03:41,660
words that end in -y.

56
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:42,980
So listen.

57
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:44,480
Ea-sy.

58
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:46,470
Ha-ppy.

59
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:49,210
That's a bit longer word.

60
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:53,860
That's two syllables, but it ends in -y.

61
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:58,210
Then we also add ending

62
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:00,250
-er. Easier.

63
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:02,080
Happier.

64
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:07,150
But pay attention y this last letter

65
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:13,580
y would change to i and then we'll just add the ending

66
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:15,110
-er, right.

67
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:17,019
Easier.

68
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:19,000
Happier.

69
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:21,630
Okay.

70
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,280
One more thing about the short words.

71
00:04:25,770 --> 00:04:27,300
For example, the word big.

72
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:34,980
So in the word big, when we add -er, we will write double g.

73
00:04:36,030 --> 00:04:43,050
So if the if the word is short, it ends in one vowel i and one consonant g,

74
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:50,130
then the last letter, the last consonant is doubled. Bigger.

75
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:57,150
So bigger is the most common example of this rule.

76
00:04:57,420 --> 00:05:07,680
So just remember bigger and you're going to be fine. Those were short words. Now longer, for example,

77
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:09,960
accurate.

78
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:11,600
A-ccu-rate.

79
00:05:11,610 --> 00:05:14,700
This is a longer word, three syllables.

80
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:18,600
We counted three syllables. With longer words

81
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:21,390
we don't add any endings.

82
00:05:21,390 --> 00:05:23,400
They are long already.

83
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,330
Yes, we just add a word before them.

84
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:30,420
And this would be the word more.

85
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:33,300
This is more accurate, right

86
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,430
This is more accurate.

87
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:44,270
But if we want to compare it, like if you want to to do a reverse comparing, right.

88
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:47,520
Reverse, we can say less.

89
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:48,120
Right.

90
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:50,190
This is less accurate.

91
00:05:50,190 --> 00:05:51,570
This is more accurate.

92
00:05:51,570 --> 00:05:54,660
This is less accurate. Here

93
00:05:54,660 --> 00:05:56,070
it's possible to do so.

94
00:05:56,130 --> 00:05:58,950
And for example, the word advanced.

95
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:01,890
Advanced, right.

96
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:05,170
Ad-vanced.

97
00:06:06,070 --> 00:06:08,770
So here we have two syllables.

98
00:06:09,770 --> 00:06:16,090
But it doesn't end in y so we don't add ending.

99
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:20,480
We just add more or less. More advanced.

100
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:21,830
Less advanced.

101
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:23,580
Okay.

102
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:29,610
So remember that with two syllable words we add endings only if they end in 

103
00:06:29,610 --> 00:06:34,560
y. Easy, happy, but more advanced.

104
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:36,630
Okay.

105
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:40,210
And as always in English, we have exceptions.

106
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:43,120
But don't worry, here we don't have a lot.

107
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:50,110
But there are a few words that do not add the ending 

108
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:53,270
-er, they do not use the word more, they

109
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:55,750
they are just changed completely.

110
00:06:56,590 --> 00:06:58,960
And those words are: good

111
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:09,450
and the comparative is better, than bad and the comparative is worse and far

112
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:12,480
and the comparative is further.

113
00:07:14,450 --> 00:07:15,080
Okay.

114
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:16,160
So this is it.

115
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:19,910
We covered comparative objectives.

116
00:07:20,910 --> 00:07:23,550
And now let's look at some examples.

117
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:25,230
Okay.

118
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:26,020
Here we go.

119
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:28,900
I like this UI.

120
00:07:28,930 --> 00:07:31,390
It's cleaner than the previous one.

121
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,590
So it's cleaner.

122
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:41,040
And when we compare two things, we usually use this word

123
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:46,150
than. It's cleaner than the previous one.

124
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:48,190
Pay attention

125
00:07:48,190 --> 00:07:50,290
it's written through a.

126
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:54,570
You have to write it just like this - than.

127
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:56,980
Okay.

128
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:57,940
The next one.

129
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:04,090
Let's start with the bigger stuff and then we can focus on the smaller details.

130
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:07,110
Bigger stuff.

131
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:12,180
And then so this is a completely different word.

132
00:08:12,210 --> 00:08:13,620
It means later.

133
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:16,290
Do not confuse it with this one.

134
00:08:17,490 --> 00:08:20,550
Than and then are different.

135
00:08:22,790 --> 00:08:23,460
Okay.

136
00:08:23,570 --> 00:08:28,760
The next one, Alex's code is easier to read than Jay's.

137
00:08:31,210 --> 00:08:33,010
Easier to read.

138
00:08:37,549 --> 00:08:38,450
Next one.

139
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:44,720
Most air traffic control systems are less advanced than the technology found in smartphones.

140
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:47,720
Less advanced.

141
00:08:48,110 --> 00:08:50,750
So we compare it, but we use the word less.

142
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:53,300
The next one.

143
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:57,340
This task description is more accurate.

144
00:08:58,360 --> 00:08:59,590
More accurate.

145
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:06,260
And the last one. Before we go further, please read the specifications.

146
00:09:08,710 --> 00:09:09,280
Okay.

147
00:09:10,940 --> 00:09:11,510
Great.

148
00:09:11,510 --> 00:09:15,590
So we covered some examples and we move on.

149
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:22,420
Now we are going to talk about comparing adverbs.

150
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:26,710
Remember, adverbs describe actions.

151
00:09:26,950 --> 00:09:27,730
Right.

152
00:09:28,670 --> 00:09:31,460
To work slowly.

153
00:09:31,790 --> 00:09:33,530
That's what we had at the beginning.

154
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:41,210
As I said, as I mentioned at the beginning, we make those adverbs adding ending -ly.

155
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:42,530
Careful.

156
00:09:43,010 --> 00:09:44,210
Carefully.

157
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:45,680
Slow.

158
00:09:45,680 --> 00:09:46,940
Slowly.

159
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:52,070
Which means they are already pretty long words.

160
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:52,860
Right?

161
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:56,190
And with adverbs, we will

162
00:09:57,260 --> 00:10:04,970
always use more for comparing - more carefully, more slowly.

163
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:09,990
We would not say slowlier, no, more carefully, more slowly.

164
00:10:09,990 --> 00:10:12,540
That's easier you don't have to think about it.

165
00:10:13,710 --> 00:10:15,780
But just a few exceptions.

166
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:22,950
Look, some adverbs sound the same as adjectives, right

167
00:10:23,340 --> 00:10:27,810
So we don't add -ly, we have hard, fast,

168
00:10:27,810 --> 00:10:28,590
long.

169
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:30,260
We don't say longly,

170
00:10:30,270 --> 00:10:31,290
we don't say fastly,

171
00:10:31,370 --> 00:10:31,680
no.

172
00:10:32,310 --> 00:10:34,090
They just stay the same.

173
00:10:34,110 --> 00:10:35,040
Right.

174
00:10:35,610 --> 00:10:38,490
So with those we will add

175
00:10:38,490 --> 00:10:44,130
And in -er  harder, faster, longer. To work

176
00:10:44,130 --> 00:10:48,240
faster, to take longer, it takes longer.

177
00:10:49,350 --> 00:10:52,530
And those exceptions the same again.

178
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:56,490
Also important, we can say something is good.

179
00:10:56,490 --> 00:10:59,930
For example, good job, right

180
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:02,820
But we say to do something well.

181
00:11:03,930 --> 00:11:05,730
Well. So,

182
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,070
Well - better

183
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:09,180
Badly -

184
00:11:09,180 --> 00:11:10,320
worse.

185
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:12,450
Far - further.

186
00:11:13,470 --> 00:11:15,630
So these forms here

187
00:11:15,630 --> 00:11:19,200
comparatives are the same as adjectives.

188
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:21,610
Okay.

189
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:23,260
This is it

190
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:24,760
about the adverbs.

191
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:28,340
And let's look at the examples.

192
00:11:28,820 --> 00:11:32,210
Please read the specifications more carefully.

193
00:11:34,270 --> 00:11:39,370
This is an old laptop, so the compiler runs more slowly.

194
00:11:41,210 --> 00:11:43,520
I work harder than him.

195
00:11:44,660 --> 00:11:47,570
She speaks English better than me.

196
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:50,380
All right.

197
00:11:50,380 --> 00:11:56,170
So these are the examples for adverbs, comparing adverbs.

198
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,220
And we move on.

199
00:11:59,670 --> 00:12:00,270
All right.

200
00:12:00,270 --> 00:12:05,010
So here we'll talk a little about modifiers of comparisons.

201
00:12:05,970 --> 00:12:13,280
So sometimes we want to say it's a lot bigger and sometimes we want to say it just a bit bigger.

202
00:12:13,290 --> 00:12:14,010
Right.

203
00:12:14,310 --> 00:12:17,370
So we want to add some tone to that.

204
00:12:17,370 --> 00:12:17,790
Right.

205
00:12:17,790 --> 00:12:19,140
Some little detail.

206
00:12:20,150 --> 00:12:23,320
And for that we can use those words.

207
00:12:23,330 --> 00:12:29,360
So if we talk about a small difference, we will use these ones right here.

208
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:33,600
A bit, slightly, a little.

209
00:12:35,730 --> 00:12:40,320
If it's somewhere in the middle, we might say

210
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:42,750
Not much, rather.

211
00:12:44,050 --> 00:12:49,660
And if the difference is really big, we would say a lot,

212
00:12:49,720 --> 00:12:53,650
far, much and way more.

213
00:12:53,890 --> 00:12:55,270
Way more.

214
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:59,740
That's something you would rather use in speaking.

215
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:01,330
So it's like informal.

216
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:03,880
Look at the examples.

217
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:10,570
So here we have the same things, the same sentences we had, but now we added those modifiers.

218
00:13:11,370 --> 00:13:12,880
I like this UI.

219
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:15,750
It's a bit cleaner than the previous one.

220
00:13:15,780 --> 00:13:16,860
A bit cleaner.

221
00:13:18,110 --> 00:13:22,340
Alex's code is slightly easier to read than Jay's.

222
00:13:24,650 --> 00:13:32,270
Most air traffic control systems are far less advanced than the technology found in smartphones.

223
00:13:33,700 --> 00:13:35,260
So the difference is big.

224
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:39,420
This test description is way more accurate.

225
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:41,640
Much more accurate.

226
00:13:43,230 --> 00:13:45,690
That's rather more than we have planned for the

227
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:46,470
Sprint.

228
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:50,590
She works not much faster than me.

229
00:13:53,780 --> 00:13:54,620
All right.

230
00:13:54,950 --> 00:14:01,700
So here are some words that can help you, to be more precise, when you compare something.

231
00:14:01,850 --> 00:14:03,360
Here we go, now

232
00:14:03,410 --> 00:14:05,870
we'll talk about superlatives.

233
00:14:06,170 --> 00:14:09,920
So we can say that something is bigger,

234
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:13,070
but then something is the biggest.

235
00:14:13,100 --> 00:14:14,960
The biggest in the group.

236
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:17,360
So the biggest in the group.

237
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:19,610
The best, number one.

238
00:14:19,610 --> 00:14:22,010
That's superlative, right

239
00:14:22,010 --> 00:14:23,360
The highest degree.

240
00:14:25,530 --> 00:14:28,440
How do we make those superlatives?

241
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:31,550
So the rules are the same.

242
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:34,720
Shorter words, longer words.

243
00:14:34,740 --> 00:14:37,440
It all applies here as well.

244
00:14:37,770 --> 00:14:45,720
It's just we don't add ending -er as in comparatives, but ending -est

245
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:48,630
So the cleanest.

246
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:50,580
Clean, cleaner,

247
00:14:50,580 --> 00:14:51,810
the cleanest.

248
00:14:53,080 --> 00:15:02,380
Then big, bigger, the biggest and you see double g just as we already talked.

249
00:15:02,380 --> 00:15:06,410
Right then easy,

250
00:15:06,490 --> 00:15:08,620
Easier, the easiest.

251
00:15:09,590 --> 00:15:16,400
Again we just add -est and change y to i.

252
00:15:16,430 --> 00:15:18,650
So the rules are the same.

253
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:21,570
Just pay attention.

254
00:15:21,590 --> 00:15:32,450
One thing that is special about superlative, we use it with article the - the cleanest, the biggest.

255
00:15:32,450 --> 00:15:33,200
Right.

256
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:36,830
So it makes them unique in a group.

257
00:15:36,830 --> 00:15:46,850
That's why article the. And instead of the we can sometimes say my, right, my, your, their, right.

258
00:15:46,850 --> 00:15:54,440
If you say my best project, then then you can omit the, you say my instead of it.

259
00:15:55,770 --> 00:15:56,250
Okay.

260
00:15:56,370 --> 00:15:58,920
With the longer words, the same.

261
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:02,490
The most accurate.

262
00:16:02,490 --> 00:16:04,950
Accurate, more accurate,

263
00:16:04,950 --> 00:16:10,500
the most accurate. Advanced. Advanced,

264
00:16:10,950 --> 00:16:12,390
Less advanced.

265
00:16:12,570 --> 00:16:14,430
The least advanced.

266
00:16:14,850 --> 00:16:19,290
So this was the comparing things not up, but down.

267
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:21,780
Less advanced.

268
00:16:21,810 --> 00:16:23,880
The least advanced.

269
00:16:24,990 --> 00:16:27,930
And the exceptions are the same.

270
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:30,480
The best, the worst.

271
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:31,920
The furthest.

272
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:33,810
Good, better,

273
00:16:33,810 --> 00:16:34,860
the best.

274
00:16:35,130 --> 00:16:35,910
